body:
Hello all,

I believe I've seen some similar proposal somewhere, but cannot seem to find it. If I recall correctly @domenic talked about it in some thread.

The use-case for this is my case would be styling based on attributes. For example, I have a custom element <my-foo></my-foo> inside another custom element <my-bar></my-bar>, I set some attribute, lets say `invalid` and based on that I want to apply some styles.
The HTML for this example would be:
```HTML
<my-bar invalid>
  #shadow-root
    <my-foo></my-foo>
</my-bar>
```

To pass the attribute to `my-foo` from `my-bar`, I would need to define `observedAttributes` array and attach some logic in JS to add or remove that attribute (the more nested levels -> more duplicated logic).

Another solution would be to use `host-context` but that's like a parent selector in CSS, and I doubt that we will see it.

I'm not familiar with all the internals of the browsers, but I guess introducing something like a special token for inherited attributes is likely to be rejected as it would potentially require a change in HTML parser, for example: `<my-foo &invalid>` (`&` here means inheritance).
The only option that I see is moving that special token into the value itself `<my-foo invalid="&inherit">` or even `<my-foo invalid="&">`.

So instead of doing this:
```JS
static get observedAttributes() {
	return ['invalid'];
}
attributeChangedCallback(attrName, oldVal, newVal) {
	if (attrName === 'invalid') {
		const myFoo = this.shadowRoot.querySelector('my-foo');
		if (this.hasAttribute('invalid')) {
			myFoo.setAttribute('invalid', '');
		} else {
			myFoo.removeAttribute('invalid');
		}
	}
}
```
I would do this:
```HTML
<my-bar invalid>
  #shadow-root
    <my-foo &invalid></my-foo>
</my-bar>
```

While it might be possible to set such attribute for every element if you have control over everything, but not when developing a component that will be used in other projects by different people.
